# Prove a classification of a PRIVATE number, revealing only the verdict WORD, not the number.
# Default proofs are sound but not zero-knowledge: add --zk to hide the private inputs.
#
#   glass prove examples/prove/private_verdict.glass score=820   # -> "PASS", ACCEPT
#   glass prove examples/prove/private_verdict.glass score=640   # -> "WAIT", ACCEPT
#   glass prove examples/prove/private_verdict.glass score=500   # -> "FAIL", ACCEPT
#
# The result is a STRING chosen by a private comparison. A function returning a string lowers to a
# multi-wire value, and an `if`/`else` over strings muxes them wire-by-wire. Here every verdict is a
# tidy 4 characters, but the branches need NOT be equal width: see private_risk.glass for an
# `if` over words of different lengths (the bridge NUL-pads the shorter). The proof reveals the verdict word and binds it
# as the public result; the score it was computed from stays in the witness. The cross-check
# (glass.py) recomputes the same word: proving the circuit selected the verdict the source dictates,
# not some corrupted muxing of the branches.
fn verdict(s: Int) : String =
  if s >= 750 then "PASS"
  else if s >= 600 then "WAIT"
  else "FAIL"

verdict(score)
